answers.suggestions:
1) Give the moderator a state panel showing:
 - each player's role, traits and remaining tokens;
 - Bounty Hunter and Executioner targets;
 - who has won, lost or changed role, and when.
2) Add one clause each to the Bounty Hunter and Executioner text:
 - a shared fatal kill counts as "died by your attack";
 - a Bounty Hunter who dies and can no longer win loses silently;
 - the Executioner-to-Jester message goes out at dawn.
3) Rename phases to make the order obvious, e.g. "Night 3 (before Day 3)" or "Night after Day 3".
4) Show players a short reminder of common mistakes, such as "Veteran alert is not a visit" and "Protectors and protected players get no attack feedback except the attacked player's own message", or let the moderator post neutral citations of rules.
5) Use the same wording for repeated result messages, and send a player's own attack result before the bounty or other knock-on messages.
6) Consider making messages cost more when they repeat earlier content. Repetition hurt Day 2 more than Days 3 and 4.
